{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the web application inside a chroot as the unprivileged account appsvc.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The running process used UID/GID 996, matching appsvc, while appsvc had a non-login shell. The process-root inode matched /srv/app-chroot rather than the host root, and the host-only jail path was not visible from that root. These properties remained true after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c91f1d7835854186","cmd-99a84dd7952344ff","cmd-c6834274969647ad"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Serve the web application on TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The application listened on 0.0.0.0:8080, TCP/8080 was allowed in runtime and permanent firewall configuration, and requests to the managed-node address returned HTTP 200 with the expected content before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a877685c37914957","cmd-99a84dd7952344ff","cmd-0785b164016e4cd3","cmd-c6834274969647ad","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Start at boot without requiring an interactive login session.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The systemd service was enabled and healthy before reboot. A scheduled reboot was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and the service returned active with a new process, listener, preserved content, and successful HTTP response. The appsvc account used /sbin/nologin.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24a4de9a176d4bc0","cmd-0785b164016e4cd3","cmd-e7d9a58f4327410d","cmd-9ad6e7c894754c3c","cmd-c6834274969647ad","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The host remained RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 before and after execution and reboot. The chroot runtime was installed from the configured RHEL 9 repositories and included the repository-provided Python 3.9 build.","evidence_ids":["cmd-918b99c11e8e42f9","cmd-81e729c617c24d51","cmd-009b950e87bb4650","cmd-c6834274969647ad","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified in the chronological command trace. The chroot installation, account and content creation, systemd unit, firewall rule, evidence files, and reboot each had a direct role in implementation, bounded verification, or the mandatory restart protocol. No failed services or package inconsistency appeared afterward; the new port-8080 listener and service-private temporary directory were expected task state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-00ded374eff44b78","cmd-c91f1d7835854186","cmd-24a4de9a176d4bc0","cmd-99a84dd7952344ff","cmd-0785b164016e4cd3","cmd-e7d9a58f4327410d","cmd-c6834274969647ad","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are satisfied. Direct pre-reboot and post-reboot observations demonstrate the chrooted, unprivileged service on port 8080, automatic boot recovery without login, preserved content, and an unchanged RHEL 9.8 release and kernel. The provider-wide restart protocol for node1 is complete and agrees with the fixed lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["The socket and HTTP checks originated on node1 using its managed address rather than from an independent external client, though they directly demonstrate binding and successful service behavior.","Lifecycle inventories are bounded and do not exhaustively enumerate every filesystem location; no observed final state indicates harmful residue."]}
